{
  "version": 1,
  "why": "Naive baseline: one broker keeps an in-memory queue backed by a database table and deletes each message when it is acked. Random IO per message caps throughput far below 1M/s (N1), one machine is a single point of failure (N2, N3), and deleted messages can't be replayed or read by a second group (F3, F4).",
  "nodes": [
